            "kind": "REWORK",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 28,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "Please document why we don't see these SSL_set1_host calls any other examples of other well reviewed HTTP clients."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 28,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "(in your commit message)"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 28,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "garybeihl",
                        "email": "garybeihl@microsoft.com",
                        "username": "garybeihl"
                    },
                    "message": "Done in PS4 \u2014 swapped SSL_set1_host for Boost Asio's ssl::host_name_verification registered via set_verify_callback). Rationale documented in the updated commit message: both reach the same OpenSSL primitive (X509_check_host); the Asio wrapper is the idiomatic form and is what Boost documentation points to."
                },
                {
                    "file": "http/http_client.hpp",
                    "line": 627,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "When I make a comparison to libcurl, which I would expect is more reviewed than this, I see no calls to SSL_set1_host.  I do see calls to SSL_set_tlsext_host_name.  Is there an example here, or documentation we can use to verify this is the correct way to write this code?\n\nI also don't see this in the beast SSL examples.  Is everyone else just wrong here, or is there something else going on?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "http/http_client.hpp",
                    "line": 627,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "@priya_irudayaraj@yahoo.co.in\n\nYou also recently added this in your patch.  Why is this suddenly coming up?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "http/http_client.hpp",
                    "line": 627,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "garybeihl",
                        "email": "garybeihl@microsoft.com",
                        "username": "garybeihl"
                    },
                    "message": "Done in PS4. The code now uses ssl::host_name_verification via set_verify_callback. Commit msg notes that libcurl reaches the same check via 509_VERIFY_PARAM_set1_host \u2014 SSL_set1_host is a thin alias, and host_name_verification ends up calling X509_check_host under the hood. Three names, one check; libcurl is not skipping hostname verification."
                },
                {
                    "file": "http/http_client.hpp",
                    "line": 627,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "priyair",
                        "email": "priya_irudayaraj@yahoo.co.in",
                        "username": "priyair"
                    },
                    "message": "@ed@tanous.net, As per your commment, RFC\u202f6066 defines SNI for DNS names, but does not forbid IP addresses. Redfish aggregation satellites are typically addressed by IP, and OpenBMC\u2019s implementation uses IPs for satellite connections. \nSSL_set_tlsext_host_name() allows both hostnames and IP addresses. Passing an IP is valid in OpenSSL. Is there a scope to add Ipaddress?"
                }
            ],
